## Context
Raid f642df (2026-10-28): the orchestrator created its session worktree as
`t1326-$PPID` (`rules/claude-code.md` § Entering the session). Its coder
subagent ran hunt § Worktree, whose `P=$PPID` resolves to the same runtime
process, so it computed the same path. It found the tree clean, treated it as
its own and switched it to the PR branch; `raid_land.py --cleanup` then
removed it, leaving the orchestrator's shell in a deleted directory. Nothing
was lost only because every change was committed: a dirty orchestrator tree
would have been silently taken over or blocked.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] A subagent's hunt never resolves to the path of a worktree its spawner (or a sibling) holds; test with two callers sharing one `$PPID`
- [ ] An existing tree at the computed path that the caller did not create is not adopted silently (hunt's "Owned already?" test)
- [ ] `make check` passes
